course: politics
lesson: 24
title: Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On 24 February 2022 Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, sending soldiers, tanks and planes across the border, as widely reported around the world. Millions of Ukrainians had to flee their homes.
explain: Most countries and the UN General Assembly called it an invasion that broke the rules countries agreed to live by, and asked Russia to withdraw. Russia's government called it a «special military operation» and said it was protecting people and its own security.
explain: Think of a playground: whatever else two children argue about, who crossed into the other's space is a fact you can check. Here the widely reported fact is that Russia's army crossed into Ukraine; the big arguments are about how the war should end.
explain: In one place it started earlier: in 2014 Russian forces took control of Crimea, and most of the world, in a UN vote, said Crimea is still part of Ukraine.
explain: Words from the scenes: «wicked» (New England) = very; «bostin'» (Black Country) = brilliant, «ar» = yes; «tamping» (Wales) = furious; «scrapping» = fighting; «eh» (Canada) = right?
fact: On 24 February 2022 Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, as widely reported, and millions of Ukrainians fled their homes.
fact: Russia's government called it a «special military operation» and said it was protecting people and its own security; most countries rejected that reason.
fact: On 2 March 2022 the UN General Assembly called on Russia to withdraw its troops: 141 countries voted yes, 5 voted no and 35 abstained, which means they chose not to vote yes or no, news reports said.
fact: In 2014 Russian forces took control of Crimea and Russia said it had joined Russia after a vote; most countries and a UN General Assembly vote said Crimea is still part of Ukraine.
fact: During the war Ukrainian cities have often been hit by missiles and drones, and people use sirens and alarm apps to go to shelters such as basements and metro stations, it was reported.
fact: Attacks on power stations caused power cuts in Ukrainian winters, news reports said.
fact: Several rounds of peace talks were reported, including in 2025 with the United States helping, and prisoners were exchanged, but at the time of writing reports said the fighting had not ended.
fact: Kyiv is the spelling that comes from Ukrainian and Kiev the older one from Russian; most big news organisations now use Kyiv.
fact: Ukraine's Kalush Orchestra won Eurovision in 2022 with a huge public vote; Russia was banned from that contest, and the 2023 contest was held in Liverpool on Ukraine's behalf.
fact: Russia sold a lot of gas and oil, especially to Europe, and after the invasion prices of gas, fuel and some foods such as wheat jumped, news reports said; Ukraine is a big grower of wheat and sunflowers.
fact: In spring 2022 Russian forces were pushed back from around Kyiv, later Ukraine took back other areas, and since then the front line has moved slowly.
fact: Russia controls about a fifth of Ukraine, according to widely reported estimates; Ukraine, the UN General Assembly and most countries say that land is still Ukraine.
fact: People argue about how to end the war: some say make a deal quickly even if Ukraine loses land; others say land taken by force must not be rewarded.
exercise: What happened on 24 February 2022, as widely reported? | Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine | Ukraine launched a full-scale invasion of Russia | Ukraine and Russia both joined NATO | The two countries signed a peace deal in Paris
exercise: Why did Oisin see blue and yellow flags in Irish windows in 2022? | People were showing support for Ukraine | It was the day of a big football final in Dublin | Blue and yellow are the colours of Ireland's own flag | The shops were having a big sale on flags
exercise: What did Russia's government call the invasion? | A «special military operation» | A friendly football tournament between neighbours | A peace mission carried out for NATO | A summer holiday trip for its soldiers
exercise: What did the UN General Assembly vote of 2 March 2022 call for? | Russia to stop and withdraw its troops from Ukraine | Ukraine to give Crimea to Russia | Every country in the UN to send soldiers to help Russia | The UN to close down
exercise: About how many countries voted yes in that vote? | 141 | 5 | 35 | 193
exercise: What does «abstain» mean in a vote? | Choosing not to vote yes or no | Voting twice for the same thing to make it count more | Voting for both sides at the same time | Leaving the UN for good and never coming back
exercise: What happened in Crimea in 2014? | Russian forces took control and Russia said it had joined Russia | Crimea floated away into the Atlantic | Ukraine sold Crimea to Turkey for a large amount of money | Crimea left both countries and joined the European Union
exercise: What do most countries and a UN vote say about Crimea? | It is still part of Ukraine | It has always been part of France, ever since 1945 | It is now its own country, recognised by every member of the UN | Nobody has any view about it at all
exercise: What does Daryna's gran in Kharkiv do when the alarm app buzzes? | Goes down to the basement to shelter | Goes outside to watch the sky and see what is coming | Turns off the phone and just ignores it | Drives straight to the border without stopping to pack
exercise: Mateo asks if the war is over because of peace talks. What did the latest reports say? | Talks happened, but the fighting had not ended | The war ended in 2022, a few weeks after it started | There were never any talks | Both sides agreed on everything, so the fighting stopped
exercise: Which spelling of Ukraine's capital comes from Ukrainian and is now used by most news? | Kyiv | Kiev | Kief | Kiew
exercise: Who won Eurovision 2022? | Ukraine's Kalush Orchestra | Russia's entry, by a long way | The UK's entry, which won by a mile | Nobody, because it was cancelled that year
exercise: Why did gas and some food prices jump after the invasion? | Supplies were cut and countries stopped buying Russian gas | The war made the Sun hotter | Shops raised prices just for fun, all at the same time | Gas became illegal in Europe
exercise: A boy says «they're both as bad, they're always fighting». What is the checkable fact? | Russia's army crossed into Ukraine in February 2022 | Ukraine's army invaded Russia in February 2022 | The two countries are not neighbours, so the armies never met | Nobody knows who crossed the border
exercise: What does the red shaded part on Aroha's news map show? | Land Russian forces control, as reported | Places where it is raining that day across Ukraine | Areas with the most sunflowers | Land Ukraine chose to sell to Russia for money
exercise: About how much of Ukraine does Russia control, by widely reported estimates? | About a fifth | All of it | About half | None at all
test: A boy in Boston says: «wicked confusing, my grandpa says Kiev and my teacher writes Kyiv, who's wrong?» What is the best answer? | Kyiv comes from Ukrainian and is now standard; Kiev is the older spelling from Russian | Kiev is the only correct spelling | They are two different cities a long way apart | Kyiv is a spelling mistake that the teacher keeps making, and Grandpa's spelling is the only right one
test: Hannah's grandad from the Black Country says: «that Kalush lot were bostin', but they only won 'cause of the war, ar.» Which is fair? | People may have voted for more than one reason: support for Ukraine and the song itself | The song got no votes at all | Russia won that year | Eurovision has no public vote, so only judges choose the winner, and they always pick the saddest song
test: A girl in Cardiff says: «Mum's tamping, the gas bill's doubled. How can a war that far away do that?» What is the link? | Russia sold much of Europe's gas, supplies were cut, and prices jumped | The war switched off the Sun over Europe for a whole winter, so everyone had to burn more gas to keep warm | Wales gets all its gas from Kharkiv | The bill went up because Wales joined the war
test: In the playground a boy sneers: «shut up, they're both as bad as each other, always scrapping.» What fact comes first? | Russia's army invaded Ukraine in 2022; people argue about how the war should end | Ukraine invaded Russia first in 2022 | Nobody knows who crossed the border | There is no war at all
test: A game map shows Crimea coloured as Russia; Ethan's map shows it in stripes. Why? | Russia says Crimea is Russian since 2014; most countries and a UN vote say it is Ukraine | Crimea moves between countries every year | Map makers just guess the colours | Crimea is a small island in the Pacific Ocean, far from both countries, so nobody knows whose it is
test: What would happen to the red shaded area on the map if one army pushed the other back? | Its shape would change, because it shows land each side controls | Nothing, because it shows rainfall | It would turn blue for the summer and red again in winter, whatever the armies did on the ground | It would show only sunflower fields
test: Why do families in Ukraine use air-raid alarm apps? | They warn of missiles or drones so people can take shelter | They remind people to go shopping before the shops close | They play soft music at bedtime so that children fall asleep faster | They count the sunflowers growing in the fields
test: A friend says: «the war started in 2022, nothing happened before that.» How do you correct it? | Russian forces took Crimea in 2014; the full-scale invasion came in 2022 | The war started in 1066 | Ukraine started it in 2010 by invading Crimea | Nothing has happened at all
test: A boy in Toronto asks: «peace talks again, eh? so the war's done?» What is the careful answer? | Talks were reported, but the latest news said the fighting had not stopped | Yes, the war ended in 2022 | No talks have ever happened | The UN ended the war with one vote in New York, so it is completely over and the soldiers have gone home
test: Why did some of the 35 countries abstain in the 2 March 2022 vote, by their own account? | They said they wanted to stay neutral or friendly with both sides | They all forgot to come to the meeting that day | They were not UN members | They were not allowed to vote
test: Which statement fairly shows the main views on how to end the war? | Some say make a deal fast even if land is lost; others say land taken by force must not be rewarded | Everyone agrees on how to end it | Nobody on either side wants the war to end | Both governments want to give all land away
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the events of 24 February 2022. | The Russian Federation commenced a full-scale invasion of Ukraine | Ukraine commenced a full-scale invasion of the Russian Federation | The two states concluded a treaty of perpetual peace | Both states acceded to the North Atlantic Treaty Organization
exam: State the outcome of the vote of the General Assembly of the United Nations on 2 March 2022. | One hundred and forty-one in favour, five against and thirty-five abstentions | Five in favour, one hundred and forty-one against and thirty-five abstentions | One hundred and ninety-three in favour and none against | Thirty-five in favour, five against and one hundred and forty-one abstentions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the status of Crimea as regarded by most states? | Crimea, over which Russian forces assumed control in 2014, remains part of Ukraine | Crimea has constituted part of the French Republic since 1945 | Crimea is an independent state recognised by all members of the United Nations | Crimea was transferred voluntarily by Ukraine to Turkey
exam: Identify the spelling of the capital of Ukraine that is derived from the Ukrainian language. | Kyiv | Kiev | Kief | Kiew
